Webhook is a way for one system to automatically notify another system when a specific event happens, usually by sending an HTTP request to a URL provided by the receiving system.

Webhook = “When something happens, call this URL and tell me.”

Simple example

Suppose your application uses a payment provider.

Without a webhook, your application might repeatedly ask:

App → Payment Service: "Payment completed?"
App → Payment Service: "Payment completed?"
App → Payment Service: "Payment completed?"

This is polling.

With a webhook:

User makes payment
       ↓
Payment Provider
       ↓
Payment successful
       ↓
POST /webhooks/payment
       ↓
Your Server
       ↓
Update order → PAID

Your application gives the payment provider a webhook URL, for example:

POST /webhooks/payment

When payment succeeds, the provider sends something like:

{
  "event": "payment.success",
  "orderId": "12345",
  "amount": 2000
}

Your server receives it and processes the event.

Webhook vs Polling
Polling	Webhook
Client repeatedly asks for status	Server/provider notifies automatically
Pull-based	Push/event-driven
Many unnecessary requests possible	Request occurs when event happens
Updates may be delayed by polling interval	Usually near real-time
Important HLD points

For now, remember these:

Webhooks are event-driven callbacks over HTTP
Usually the receiver exposes an HTTP endpoint
Common in payments, GitHub events, order updates, third-party integrations
Webhooks may be delivered more than once, so handlers should generally be idempotent
Verify webhook authenticity, e.g. using signatures/secrets
Failed deliveries are commonly retried